  "brief": {
    "situation": "A month in the city's coffee market. Households in five neighborhoods (Downtown, Riverside, Eastside, Northgate, University) buy coffee from a handful of cafés. Each café has a list price, a weekly promotion, a loyalty subscription (monthly fee for a per-cup discount), a daily cup capacity and a public star rating built from customer reviews.",
    "rules": "Every day each household decides whether to buy coffee and where; a household buys one cup per member who wants one. Price matters more to lower-income households, distance matters to everyone, ratings and friends' habits spread by word of mouth. A café that has hit its daily capacity turns customers away. Every Monday (days 8, 15, 22 …) cafés see last week's results and may change price, promotion and subscription terms; changes apply from that day on.",
    "roles": {
      "household": "You are a household deciding on today's coffee. You want good coffee you can afford, close by; a subscription pays off only if you visit the same café often.",
      "cafe": "You run a café. You want the most profit over the month (revenue minus cost of cups sold) without losing your regulars. Capacity is fixed: filling it at a good price beats discounting into a queue."
    }
  },
  "clock": {
    "rounds": "$inputs.days",
    "unit": "day",
    "start": "2026-03-02"
  },
